; saffron-dynamic-surface-pass (low): `=` on two Sym-typed values was
;   TUR-E0006: operator lookup failed for '=', got 2 arg(s), first arg type Sym
; on both back ends, while the same comparison on two `any`-held Syms answered
; pointer identity (H5). So a keyword could be compared only for as long as the
; type checker did NOT know it was a keyword -- widening through an unannotated
; Saffron defn made it start working, which is backwards.
;
; The fix is a `=` / `not=` row for Sym in the builtin table. This is plain
; Turmeric on purpose: the hole was never Saffron-specific, and unlike `cstr`
; (where `==` on the pointer would silently disagree with `cstr-eq?`) a Sym is
; an interned record pointer whose identity IS its equality -- `Eq[Sym]` and
; `sym=?` say exactly the same thing -- so the row adds a spelling without
; changing what equality means. Compiled it is a C pointer compare; interpreted
; a Sym rides the int carrier and the operator compares that word.
;
; The last two cases are the ones that separate identity from name equality
; gone wrong: two literals spelled the same MUST compare equal (interning), and
; so must a keyword that arrived through a function parameter.

(defn tag? [k : Sym] : int
  (if (= k :a) 1 (if (not= k :b) 2 3)))

(defn id-sym [k : Sym] : Sym k)

(defn main [] : int
  (println (tag? :a))
  (println (tag? :b))
  (println (tag? :c))
  (println (= :same :same))
  (println (= (id-sym :rt) :rt))
  (println (not= (id-sym :rt) :rs))
  0)
